(5059411, 735939, 100448, '<td valign="top">this is an amazing shot! wowie!!  i''m a total amateur photog.  the photos on this site are totally a cut above anything i could do.  did you bring lights to this site?  how was this photo processed?  did you use photoshop or something?  if so, what effects were used?  i have an canon digital rebel xt, so not a prof. camera.</td>', 'this is an amazing shot! wowie!!  i''m a total amateur photog.  the photos on this site are totally a cut above anything i could do.  did you bring lights to this site?  how was this photo processed?  did you use photoshop or something?  if so, what effects were used?  i have an canon digital rebel xt, so not a prof. camera.', '2008-11-12 10:37:44.000000', NULL, 0),
(5059464, 735156, 42013, '<td valign="top">Congrats, perfect image for the challenge.</td>', 'Congrats, perfect image for the challenge.', '2008-11-12 10:48:48.000000', NULL, 0),
(5059522, 734503, 67551, '<td valign="top">I would have rated this way higher than it got, I think it should be in the top three. It''s a little heavy handed with the saturation/"painterly" story-book aesthetic, but I like it a lot, and the aesthetic goes well with the picture. The blue spots come out so strong, they look a little odd.<br/> <br/>I''d have maybe cropped the house a little closer into the bottom left corner, but that''s just me. Good job, anyway. The lines of that collapsing house are just great- it has so much twist.</td>', 'I would have rated this way higher than it got, I think it should be in the top three. It''s a little heavy handed with the saturation/"painterly" story-book aesthetic, but I like it a lot, and the aesthetic goes well with the picture. The blue spots come out so strong, they look a little odd. I''d have maybe cropped the house a little closer into the bottom left corner, but that''s just me. Good job, anyway. The lines of that collapsing house are just great- it has so much twist.', '2008-11-12 11:05:51.000000', NULL, 0),
(5059559, 736085, 67551, '<td valign="top">This is great. I''d have thought it would score way higher. It has a lot more interest than many of the higher rated ones, and is far from the typical abandoned house or railroad car most people would guess would be the most popular subjects on this theme.<br/> <br/>Have you tried it with it rotated so the horizon''s horizontal and the cart''s at a slight angle, instead of the cart being cropped square?<br/> <br/>Anyway, great location, concept, and execution. A lot of the most imaginative and stylistic pictures can''t do so well because the contest averages all scores. For "polarizing" pictures that get a lot of 9''s and 10''s but also a lot of 3''s and 4''s because they''re interesting but rubbed some people the wrong way, they might try some sort of "honorable mention" or something that rewards images with fairly high scores and relatively high standard deviations in the scoring.</td>', 'This is great. I''d have thought it would score way higher. It has a lot more interest than many of the higher rated ones, and is far from the typical abandoned house or railroad car most people would guess would be the most popular subjects on this theme. Have you tried it with it rotated so the horizon''s horizontal and the cart''s at a slight angle, instead of the cart being cropped square? Anyway, great location, concept, and execution. A lot of the most imaginative and stylistic pictures can''t do so well because the contest averages all scores. For "polarizing" pictures that get a lot of 9''s and 10''s but also a lot of 3''s and 4''s because they''re interesting but rubbed some people the wrong way, they might try some sort of "honorable mention" or something that rewards images with fairly high scores and relatively high standard deviations in the scoring.', '2008-11-12 11:17:37.000000', NULL, 0),
(5060202, 736091, 50641, '<td valign="top">You biased the results of your experiment by using this title.  For example, I read it and thought it was tongue in cheek - making fun of the narrowminded orthodoxy on DPC who prefer the most clinically accurate photographs.  The reverse of your hypothesis is tested here all the time - how often do tack-sharp, moodless, faithful photos of obvious subjects receive ridiculously high scores? My preference is for fun, interesting warped, imperfect, moody... every time.  p.s. if you''re taking a stance, how many True Photographs are in your portfolio?</td>', 'You biased the results of your experiment by using this title.  For example, I read it and thought it was tongue in cheek - making fun of the narrowminded orthodoxy on DPC who prefer the most clinically accurate photographs.  The reverse of your hypothesis is tested here all the time - how often do tack-sharp, moodless, faithful photos of obvious subjects receive ridiculously high scores? My preference is for fun, interesting warped, imperfect, moody... every time.  p.s. if you''re taking a stance, how many True Photographs are in your portfolio?', '2008-11-12 14:50:33.000000', NULL, 0),
